---
/**
 * PageShell - D1 of the restructure plan. The `<main>` shell existed in six
 * variants across seventeen routes: section gaps of 40, 24 and 28 with top
 * padding 24, 32, 16 and 20, so the "huge vertical rhythm" law (grammar law 5)
 * was not one rhythm but four. This component owns the three named rhythms and
 * nothing else - a page picks one and stops writing padding.
 *
 * The page still writes the element: it writes `<PageShell>`, and the component
 * emits the `<main>`. That is what keeps the HEAD CONTRACT's "every page's slot
 * content is wrapped in `<main>` BY THE PAGE" true, since the page is the one
 * that places it. The deep-HTML sweep greps the BUILT tree for `<main>`, so
 * verify in `Target/`, never in the page file.
 *
 * ── THE BLUEPRINT RULING (Stitch grammar) ────────────────────────────────
 *
 * The shell also owns the two things that make a page read as a drawing rather
 * than as a stack of blocks:
 *
 *   1. THE RULING. A quiet grid behind the content column - 40px cells in
 *      `--blueprint-rule` at 35% - so the eye can measure the page and the
 *      space between plates stops being empty. It is drawn INSIDE the shell's
 *      own box, not across the window: the ruling belongs to the content
 *      measure (1440px), and a grid that ran to the window edge would end in a
 *      seam on every wide monitor and cost a `100vw` box that clips a
 *      scrollbar.
 *
 *   2. THE CHAPTER RULE. Every `<section>` that is a DIRECT child of the shell
 *      opens with a full-width hairline and the short drop from that rule to
 *      its first line. This is the numbered-divider rhythm: the rule is the
 *      shell's, the NUMBER is the page's own `.section-index` tag
 *      (`01 // POSITIONING`, Global.css), because a counter rendered here
 *      would have to sit in the section's flow or on a `position: relative`
 *      the section may not own - and forcing `relative` on a section re-anchors
 *      every absolutely positioned decoration inside it, which is a layout
 *      change across 64 routes to gain a digit.
 *
 * The ruling and the rules are ONE switch, `Blueprint`, and it defaults to ON
 * for a document and OFF for a viewport: `Grow` is the workbench, whose panes
 * are an application surface rather than a document, and chapter rules across
 * an app read as chrome over the tool. A page may still say `Blueprint={false}`
 * or `Blueprint` explicitly.
 *
 * BOTH SELECTORS ARE DELIBERATELY WEAK. The chapter rule is written
 * `:where(.shell--blueprint) > section`, so it carries element-level
 * specificity and every utility a page already puts on its own section wins
 * (`border-t border-oxblood-dark` keeps its crimson hairline, `pt-24` keeps its
 * padding). The shell supplies a rule only where a page states none.
 */
import "@Stylesheet/Component/Chrome.scss";

import Breadcrumbs from "./Breadcrumbs.astro";

interface Props {
	/** The section gap + page padding pair. "wide" is the default page rhythm. */
	Rhythm?: "wide" | "dense" | "hero";

	/** Render `<Breadcrumbs />` as the first child. */
	Crumbs?: boolean;

	/**
	 * Let the shell fill a non-scrolling viewport (`flex-1`). The workbench is
	 * the only page that needs it: its panes are a viewport, not a document.
	 */
	Grow?: boolean;

	/**
	 * The blueprint ruling behind the content column plus the chapter rule that
	 * opens every direct-child `<section>`. On for a document, off for a
	 * viewport surface (`Grow`). See the header for why both are one switch.
	 */
	Blueprint?: boolean;
}

const { Rhythm = "wide", Crumbs = false, Grow = false, Blueprint = !Grow } = Astro.props;

/**
 * Byte-exact from the measured shells: wide is shell A (case-study, examples,
 * hermes, plugin, tools and the rest), dense is shell B (benchmarks, setup,
 * versions), hero is shell C (the landing page's asymmetric top).
 *
 * The three rhythms are stylesheet classes now (`.shell--wide/dense/hero` in
 * Component/Chrome.scss), so a page names its rhythm and writes no padding.
 */
const RhythmClass = `shell--${Rhythm}`;
---

<main
	class:list={["shell", RhythmClass, Blueprint && "shell--blueprint", Grow && "shell--grow"]}
>
	{Crumbs && <Breadcrumbs />}
	<slot />
</main>
